<P>The candles were burning low, and the tavern was empty.</P><br><P>&#9;"…It can take the form of anything it chooses," said Kheldan.</P><br><P>&#9;"…and the voice." I added.</P><br><P>&#9;"And it feeds on sorrow—on terror."</P><br><P>&#9;"Aye, it seems to enjoy it immensely."</P><br><P>&#9;There was a long pause.</P><br><P>&#9;I cleared my throat. "Ahem…that’s it?"</P><br><P>&#9;Kheldan thought a moment and nodded, "Aye."</P><br><P>&#9;"That is not much to go on. We need to know more. Much more. The monster must have a weakness…or at least a home. Does Halas have a place of knowledge…a hall of records? Perhaps we could look at records of untimely deaths to learn more?"</P><br><P>&#9;Kheldan shook his head. "We are a people of oral tradition, Eshkin. Khondoran has already told me all he knows. We learn to write and read your language only so we can deal with outsiders. We have no need o’ such things in Halas."</P><br><P>&#9;Hmm. No wonder they called them Barbarians.</P><br><P>&#9;I thought for a moment. Kheldan was feeling the same strange elation I was; I could see it in his eyes. We were balanced on a moment of decision.</P><br><P>&#9;"Well," I said at length, "Are you up for a journey?"</P><br><P>&#9;His slow smile was answer enough. "To where?"</P><br><P>&#9;I carefully rolled and lit my last cigarette on the flame of the ebbing candle, rolling the options over in my mind. A feeling of peace settled over me as the decision was made. I knew that Tunare’s blessing was with me again.</P><br><P></P><br><CENTER>***</CENTER>         <br>
